242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (1- Domains: Active Directory organizes resources into logical domains, which are administrative boundaries within a network. Domains can include users, groups, computers, and other objects.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (2- Domain Controllers: Domain controllers are servers that run the Active Directory service and store a replica of the Active Directory database. They authenticate users and enforce security policies within their respective domains.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (3- Forests: A forest is a collection of one or more domains that share a common schema, configuration, and global catalog. It represents the top-level structure in Active Directory and provides a boundary for authentication and replication.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (4- Organizational Units \(OUs\): OUs are containers within a domain that are used to organize and manage resources. They allow administrators to delegate administrative tasks and apply group policies to specific sets of objects.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
246 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (5- Trust Relationships: Active Directory supports establishing trust relationships between domains, allowing users from one domain to access resources in another domain.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
